WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.960
If you asked a random person on the street
to choose between the S&amp;P 500 and a professionally

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.180
managed hedge fund, chances are most of them
would choose the hedge fund.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.910
And this makes sense, I mean why would you
invest into the broad market when you can

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:18.500
have hundreds of financial professionals actively
manage your money on a day to day basis.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.550
Though the opportunity sounds awfully attractive,
the average person doesn’t have access to

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.960
a hedge fund.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.400
Most hedge funds not only require that you’re
a millionaire but also that you invest $1

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.940
million into the hedge fund itself.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.930
These lofty requirements have given hedge
funds an image of being a fancy investment

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.850
tool for the filthy rich.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.650
But, when we peel back the layers, we’ll
see that hedge funds aren’t actually that

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.650
fancy.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.690
In fact, an investment into the S&amp;P 500 in
2010 would’ve produced nearly triple the

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.510
returns of hedge funds.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.130
So, here’s the truth about hedge funds'
poor returns, and why the rich levitate towards

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.809
hedge funds nonetheless.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:01.780
Before we get into the various reasons hedge
funds underperform the market, let’s first

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.570
take a look at the proof.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.380
The following table showcases the average
returns produced by hedge funds and the S&amp;P

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.079
500 every year since 2011.

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:16.579
As you can see, the hedge funds underperformed
the S&amp;P 500 every single year.

00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:21.509
On green years, they made less money and on
red years, they lost more money.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:28.100
If we do a bit of math, we’ll see that the
average annual return for hedge funds is 4.956%

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:32.689
while the average annual return for the S&amp;P
500 is 14.4%.

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:37.689
So, the S&amp;P 500 crushed hedge funds over the
past 10 years.

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:41.490
Warren Buffett even predicted this outcome
way back in 2008.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:47.740
In 2008, Warren Buffett bet $1 million that
the S&amp;P 500 would outperform hedge funds over

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.310
the next ten years.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:55.140
By 2015, hedge funds were losing so badly
to the S&amp;P 500 that Buffett’s opponent Ted

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.039
Seides actually conceded 2.5 years early.

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:03.960
So, there’s no question that hedge funds
have underperformed the market, but in reality,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.610
this is not that fair of a comparison because
there’s so many more factors that go into

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.500
running a hedge fund such as hedging.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.460
As the name suggests, hedge funds hedge their
bets meaning that they always play the market

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.050
to both sides.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.820
They could be 99% confident that Apple stock
will be higher than it is today 10 years from

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:26.090
now, but despite that, they’ll have to allocate
a portion of their portfolio to betting against

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.090
Apple.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.480
Now, they don’t have to straight up short
Apple stock or buy puts on Apple.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.940
But what they do have to do is somehow offset
the risk they take on by purchasing Apple

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:36.940
stock.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.790
And to do this, they use something called
beta.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.780
Beta is a measure of a stock’s perceived
volatility in comparison to the overall market.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:50.040
The S&amp;P 500 is the baseline, so stocks that
perform identically to the S&amp;P 500 have a

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.170
beta value of 1.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.080
Stocks that outperform the market like Apple
and Tesla have a beta value greater than 1.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.880
Meanwhile, stocks that underperform the market
but are still positive have a beta value between

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.770
0 and 1.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:08.570
And finally, stocks or positions that are
negative when the market goes up or vice versa

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.990
have a negative beta value.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:14.550
The standard in the hedge fund industry is
to maintain an overall beta value of 0 which

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.020
means that the fund is neutral to the market.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:22.610
Some hedge funds do strive for negative beta
values or even beta values above 1.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:27.880
But in most cases, the goal is to maintain
a low beta value.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.970
Considering this, most hedge funds are consistently
burning large amounts of money maintaining

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:36.710
low beta values which prevents them from benefiting
from large moves in either direction.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:40.970
Aside from hedging, hedge funds burn a lot
of money on fees.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.250
When it comes to passive index funds, the
fund managers don’t really have to do much.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.090
Every quarter a couple of companies will get
kicked out of the S&amp;P 500 and a couple will

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.220
be added.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.010
So, index fund managers will have to go ahead
and buy and sell some positions every quarter,

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.970
but that’s pretty much it.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:00.930
As a result, passive funds have extremely
small expense ratios.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:06.580
The Vanguard S&amp;P 500 fund, for instance, has
an expense ratio of only 0.03%.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.080
This means that for every $100 you invest
into the fund, only 3 cents goes to the fund

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.660
managers on an annual basis.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.951
With hedge funds on the other hand, financial
analysts and portfolio managers dedicate their

00:04:16.951 --> 00:04:19.329
entire lives to managing the fund.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:24.520
On average, individuals working at hedge funds
and investment banks work 80 to 100 hours

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.090
every single week.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:30.159
And given their massive commitment, they expect
to be well compensated.

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:34.120
The standard fee structure at hedge funds
follows the 2 and 20 rule.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.919
This rule suggests that hedge funds will earn
a management fee of 2% per year and 20% of

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:40.939
all generated profits.

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:45.970
If we have a hedge fund that produces an annual
return of 10%, the hedge fund would take 4%

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:49.789
off the top meaning that the actual investor
would only earn 6%.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:54.110
To make things worse for investors, the better
the track history of a hedge fund, the higher

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:55.669
their fees will be.

00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:59.969
On the flip side, these high fees are extremely
lucrative for hedge fund managers.

00:04:59.969 --> 00:05:04.909
In 2020, for instance, the top paid hedge
fund manager was Chase Coleman, and he earned

00:05:04.909 --> 00:05:07.300
a whopping $3 billion.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:12.360
I think you can see how such a compensation
would make a massive dent in investor’s

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.360
returns.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.090
And that brings us into the next factor holding
back hedge funds which is diversification.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:23.400
The S&amp;P 500 is known as one of the most diverse
investments available on the stock market

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.520
as it combines the gains and losses of the
top 500 publicly traded companies.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.150
But, at the end of the day, the S&amp;P 500 only
deals with one asset class which is stocks.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:38.190
Hedge funds on the other hand are able to
invest into anything and everything.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:43.569
They invest into real estate, land, derivatives,
bonds, private companies, foreign currencies

00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:45.370
and much more.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.229
While some of these investments such as derivatives
and private companies could provide greater

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:54.759
returns than the S&amp;P 500, most of these alternative
investments come nowhere close to the returns

00:05:54.759 --> 00:05:55.759
of the stock market.

00:05:55.759 --> 00:05:57.909
Take 10 year treasury bonds, for instance.

00:05:57.909 --> 00:06:02.949
Currently, a 10 year treasury bond will only
pay you 1.3% per year.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:06.460
Similarly, real estate only appreciates 3
to 5% per year.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:12.379
So, when you have 10% in bonds, 10% in real
estate, 10% cash, and so on and so forth,

00:06:12.379 --> 00:06:16.590
it’s extremely difficult to beat an index
that’s 100% stocks.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:20.259
And that’s why it’s not really fair to
compare the overall returns of hedge funds

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:21.889
with the S&amp;P 500.

00:06:21.889 --> 00:06:25.740
If you wanted a true apples to apples comparison
between the two, you would need to look at

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.589
only the stock holdings of hedge funds and
then disregard the money spent on hedging

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:31.059
the portfolio.

00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:34.879
This is a much more accurate representation
of the returns hedge funds would provide if

00:06:34.879 --> 00:06:36.979
they tried to maximize returns.

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:40.060
And, we don’t have to guess the returns
of these guys as there’s plenty of hedge

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.810
funds that specifically focus on maximizing
returns.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:47.029
And as you would guess, with all of their
expertise and insider knowledge, they crush

00:06:47.029 --> 00:06:48.319
the markets.

00:06:48.319 --> 00:06:53.919
Chuck Akre, for instance, boasts an annualized
return of 46.4% over the past 3 years.

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:59.569
Philippe Laffont achieved an annualized return
of 49.9% over the past 3 years.

00:06:59.569 --> 00:07:06.449
Basically everyone on this list boasts returns
of 30, 40, 50% year after year after year.

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:11.789
But by far the best hedge fund in history
is Renaissance Technologies’ Medallion fund.

00:07:11.789 --> 00:07:17.919
Since 1988, the Medallion fund has achieved
an annual average return of 66%.

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:24.339
Given their unbeatable returns, they charge
eye popping fees of 27%, and after fees, investors

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:25.619
only get 39%.

00:07:25.619 --> 00:07:29.599
But, that’s still way higher than the S&amp;P
500.

00:07:29.599 --> 00:07:33.729
If there are hundreds of hedge funds that
destroy the S&amp;P 500, why don’t billionaires

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:37.089
and institutions spread their money across
these high flying hedge funds?

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:42.650
Well, the answer is simple, and it comes down
to the fundamental purpose of hedge funds.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:47.710
By nature, hedge funds aren’t designed to
provide massive returns and the wealthy do

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.969
not invest into hedge funds expecting high
returns.

00:07:50.969 --> 00:07:56.520
Rather, the primary goal of hedge funds is
capital preservation, and this is what attracts

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.050
billionaires towards them.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:02.590
Here’s the thing, billionaires already have
plenty of risky investments as the only way

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:06.689
to become a billionaire is to make outsized
bets on an industry or company.

00:08:06.689 --> 00:08:12.750
90 to 95% of the wealth of most billionaires
is concentrated into one maybe two companies.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:16.559
And given that they’re a billionaire, their
investment has clearly crushed the market

00:08:16.559 --> 00:08:18.460
over the past several years.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:19.639
Take Amazon for instance.

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:24.419
If you invested $500 into Amazon when they
went public, you would have 324 shares of

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:28.710
Amazon today or about $1.184 million.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.960
That translates to an annual rate of return
of 38.23%.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.030
Considering this, the last thing Jeff Bezos
would want to do is go ahead and put the 5%

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:41.770
of his Amazon shares that he does liquidate
into a return oriented investment.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:45.770
If anything, he wants to reduce his exposure
to the market and hedge funds are often the

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.750
perfect way to accomplish this.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.140
You see, though hedge funds get obliterated
by the market during bull markets, the same

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.580
cannot be said about bear markets.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:09:00.750
The truth is, the general market not only
runs up exponentially but it also falls exponentially.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:05.740
After the dotcom bubble, the nasdaq crashed
78% from its peak and individual tech companies

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.950
like Amazon crashed even further.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:12.400
So, if you were a tech company owner in 2002,
how were you going to live when all of your

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.340
money is tied up into Amazon or Google.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:19.102
You definitely don’t want to sell your shares
at the bottom of the market after the stock

00:09:19.102 --> 00:09:21.310
just crashed 95%.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:25.180
In such a scenario, it would be really nice
to have an investment that more or less held

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:26.460
it’s value and you could withdraw from.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.070
And this is where hedge funds come into play.

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:33.350
Hedge funds don’t rise as much as the market
during bull markets, but they also don’t

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:35.890
fall as much as the market during bear markets.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:40.620
This graph comparing hedge fund and S&amp;P 500
performance since 1995 perfectly outlines

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.000
this key difference.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.910
As you can see, between 1995 and 2000 during
the dotcom bubble, the S&amp;P 500 doubled the

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.050
returns of hedge funds.

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:55.310
During the dotcom crash, the hedge funds basically
went sideways while the market got crushed

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.850
and by 2002, hedge funds took the lead.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:03.060
In the 2008 financial crisis, hedge funds
did take a hit, but the drop is only about

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.060
half as much as the drop experienced by the
markets.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:11.180
And the market didn’t overtake hedge funds
till 2013 or 11 years after they lost the

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.180
lead.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.250
The S&amp;P 500 has dominated over the past decade,
but it’s likely that during the next bear

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.980
market, we’ll see hedge funds shine once
again.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.790
This doesn’t mean that index funds are better
than hedge funds or that hedge funds are better

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:24.260
than index funds.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:29.140
The reality is that they’re completely different
tools that serve completely different purposes.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.220
Index funds are perfect for capitalizing on
strong bull markets while hedge funds are

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:37.060
perfect for generating extremely consistent
returns decade after decade.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:41.880
At the end of the day, hedge funds are quite
complex and intricate, but not in the way

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.880
you think.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.970
They’re not some secret tool that the rich
use to make billions.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:49.300
In fact, they’re more of a wealth preservation
tool.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.100
And unless you have tens of millions in the
market, you really have no need to be hedging

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.810
with hedge funds.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:58.090
Did you guys realize that hedge funds aren’t
actually as fancy as they sound?

00:10:58.090 --> 00:10:59.360
Comment that down below.

